## 4. Findings

### F-UXD-001 [P1] [Authorization] Frontend RBAC drifted from backend permissions

**Remediation status:** Fixed

**Remediation summary:**
Frontend permission constants, role templates, and route permissions were aligned with backend RBAC. Agent write actions now consult the current user principal before allowing registration, lifecycle, credential, and inventory mutations.

**Files changed:**
`packages/product-platform/frontend/src/lib/rbac.ts`, `packages/product-platform/frontend/src/lib/rbac.test.ts`, `packages/product-platform/frontend/src/features/agents/AgentsPage.tsx`, `packages/product-platform/frontend/src/features/agents/AgentsPage.test.tsx`

**Tests added or updated:**
`route RBAC keeps frontend permission constants in contract with backend RBAC`; `route RBAC matches backend role templates for compliance and observability permissions`; `AgentsPage disables agent write actions for read-only users`

**Validation performed:**
`npm test -- src/lib/rbac.test.ts src/features/agents/AgentsPage.test.tsx src/features/settings/SettingsPage.test.tsx` passed; `npm run typecheck` passed; `npm run lint` passed.

**Remaining work:**
None in UXD scope.

**Remediated by:**
AI coding agent

**Status:** Incorrect / Risky

**Current implementation:**
Before remediation, frontend RBAC did not include all backend permission names and role-template semantics. It also allowed some agent write controls to render without checking `agent:write`.

**Expected implementation:**
Frontend navigation and action visibility should be derived from the same permission contract used by backend authorization. Read-only users should not see usable mutation controls, and backend remains the source of enforcement.

**Affected files:**
`packages/product-platform/frontend/src/lib/rbac.ts`, `packages/product-platform/frontend/src/features/agents/AgentsPage.tsx`, `packages/product-platform/src/product_platform/api/rbac.py`

**Affected symbols:**
`permissions`, `rolePermissions`, `routePermissions`, `userHasPermission`, `canAccessRoute`, `AgentsPage`, `AgentRegistrationWizard`, `AgentInventory`, `AgentOperations`

**Evidence:**
The execution log records that frontend RBAC omitted backend `COMPLIANCE_WRITE`, `OBSERVABILITY_READ`, and `OBSERVABILITY_WRITE`; Compliance Admin had audit write semantics instead of compliance write semantics; `/observability` was not aligned to backend observability permission; `AgentsPage` did not use the current user principal for write action gating. Current code now includes these permission constants and uses `canWriteAgents`.

**Why this matters:**
Admin UX that disagrees with backend authorization causes confusing 403s, weakens operator trust, and makes it easy to accidentally expose mutation controls to read-only roles.

**Competitor benchmark:**
Microsoft Agent 365 and Entra Agent ID patterns require admin visibility and controls to reflect the identity and access model.

**Recommended remediation:**
1. Keep frontend permission constants in contract with backend `Permission`.
2. Add tests that fail when backend permission values are missing from frontend RBAC.
3. Gate write actions by permission, not only by route access.
4. Keep backend authorization as the enforcement layer.

**Acceptance criteria:**
1. Frontend RBAC contains every backend permission value.
2. Viewer can access read-only pages but cannot trigger write actions.
3. Compliance Admin and Platform Admin permissions match backend role templates.
4. Route and action tests cover read-only and admin users.

**Suggested tests:**
1. Frontend RBAC contract test comparing frontend permission values to backend `Permission`.
2. Agent page test proving Viewer cannot create, suspend, rotate, or decommission agents.
3. Route access tests for Viewer, Policy Admin, Security Admin, Compliance Admin, and Platform Admin.

**Risk if ignored:**
Operators see controls that later fail server-side, role semantics drift between layers, and future admin pages may accidentally expose unsafe actions.

**Remediation complexity:**
Medium

**Dependencies:**
None

**Blocks production readiness:**
Yes

**Affects stated Ophanix product promise:**
Yes

